From efc5d9715b81841bbc41a16b1edf5022a58fded7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unxed <unxed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:42:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/48] runtime: add initial GOOS=hurd port (phase 1: minimal
 cgo-based runtime)

Registers hurd/amd64 in the toolchain and adds a libc-calling runtime port
modeled on Haiku's (also cgo-based, no flat raw syscall table) and
Solaris's (glibc-style gregset_t signal context), rather than Linux's
freestanding raw-syscall model -- GNU Hurd has no stable flat syscall ABI;
nearly everything is a glibc function that internally does Mach RPC.

All constants and struct layouts (errno, signals, mmap/fcntl flags,
sigcontext/ucontext_t/mcontext_t, sem_t, pthread_t/pthread_attr_t sizes,
CLOCK_* ids) were measured on real Debian GNU/Hurd with a small C probe
(unxed/debian-hurd poc/abi_probe.c), not guessed by analogy -- Hurd's errno
values are Mach error codes (0x40000000-based), not small integers, and
its REG_* gregset layout matches glibc's generic x86_64 one (same as
Linux/Solaris) rather than Haiku's named-field mcontext.

secureMode uses the AIX-style getuid/geteuid comparison instead of
issetugid(), which glibc does not export on any port.

Low-level primitives were independently verified working on real Hurd
before this port was written: gsync_wait/gsync_wake (futex equivalent),
signal delivery, and sigaltstack+SA_SIGINFO+accurate si_addr -- see
unxed/debian-hurd RESULTS.md.

Scope: runtime package only (enough for a bare 'println' program with no
stdlib imports). syscall/os/net support is deferred to a later phase.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
