From 619214821e833dd01552885aeb685d003962eb8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unxed <unxed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:21:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/48] rt0_hurd_amd64: extract argc/argv from the stack like
 Linux, not like Haiku

osinit() now completes fully (confirmed via debug prints: getpid, page
size all correct -- the GLOB_DAT dereference fix worked). New crash was
in getGodebugEarly -> indexbytebody, walking argv_index(argv, argc+1+n)
into garbage.

readelf -h confirms the ELF entry point is _rt0_amd64_hurd itself (Hurd's
/hurd/exec loads us directly; there's no glibc _start in front). That
means argc/argv/envp/auxv arrive on the stack via the standard SysV ABI
exec() convention -- exactly like Linux's _rt0_amd64_linux, which jumps to
the generic _rt0_amd64(SB) to pop them off the stack into DI/SI before
reaching rt0_go. Haiku's _rt0_amd64_haiku instead jumps straight to
rt0_go with whatever garbage happens to be in DI/SI, which is presumably
fine there because Haiku's exec/loader ABI genuinely differs -- not
something to copy blindly for an ELF/glibc target like Hurd.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015F6Vb87BFK4j9tVQhdKr9H
---
 src/runtime/rt0_hurd_amd64.s | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/runtime/rt0_hurd_amd64.s b/src/runtime/rt0_hurd_amd64.s
index a27f2e2d..99a21230 100644
--- a/src/runtime/rt0_hurd_amd64.s
+++ b/src/runtime/rt0_hurd_amd64.s
@@ -5,7 +5,12 @@
 #include "textflag.h"
 
 TEXT _rt0_amd64_hurd(SB),NOSPLIT,$-8
-	JMP	runtime·rt0_go(SB)
+	// _rt0_amd64_hurd is the real ELF entry point (Hurd's /hurd/exec
+	// loads us directly, there is no glibc _start in front of it), so
+	// argc/argv/envp/auxv are on the stack per the standard SysV ABI
+	// exec() convention, same as Linux -- use the generic extractor
+	// rather than jumping straight to rt0_go with garbage in DI/SI.
+	JMP	_rt0_amd64(SB)
 
 TEXT _rt0_amd64_hurd_lib(SB),NOSPLIT,$0
 	JMP	_rt0_amd64_lib(SB)
